1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,929
This is a decentralized identity, living
on the Stellar network.

2
00:00:03,929 --> 00:00:08,643
This series is recorded against version
0.1.0 of the Soroban Identity SDK.

3
00:00:08,643 --> 00:00:14,536
Nobody issued it to this wallet, no
company stores it, and any app on Stellar

4
00:00:14,536 --> 00:00:17,286
can read it in a single call.

5
00:00:17,286 --> 00:00:23,179
In the next five minutes you'll see what
Soroban Identity is, why it exists, and

6
00:00:23,179 --> 00:00:24,750
how it's put together.

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:33,398
Web3 has a strange gap. Your wallet can
hold millions, but it can't say anything

8
00:00:33,398 --> 00:00:35,637
about who you are.

9
00:00:35,637 --> 00:00:43,475
Every app starts from zero. Reputation you
earn in one marketplace doesn't follow you

10
00:00:43,475 --> 00:00:45,155
to the next.

11
00:00:45,155 --> 00:00:53,553
And when an app needs compliance, like
KYC, the usual answer is to collect your

12
00:00:53,553 --> 00:01:01,951
documents and keep them on a server, which
is exactly the kind of central honeypot

13
00:01:01,951 --> 00:01:04,750
blockchains were supposed to avoid.

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:13,084
Soroban Identity is an identity layer for
Stellar, built as Soroban smart contracts.

15
00:01:13,084 --> 00:01:21,791
It gives every wallet a decentralized
identifier, a DID, in the form did colon

16
00:01:21,791 --> 00:01:26,144
stellar colon, followed by the wallet
address.

17
00:01:26,144 --> 00:01:34,228
Trusted issuers can attach verifiable
credentials to that DID: a KYC badge, a

18
00:01:34,228 --> 00:01:37,337
proof of membership, an achievement.

19
00:01:37,337 --> 00:01:43,556
Apps then verify those credentials
on-chain, without ever seeing the

20
00:01:43,556 --> 00:01:45,422
underlying personal data.

21
00:01:45,422 --> 00:01:52,262
And a reputation layer lets trusted
reporters score on-chain activity, with

22
00:01:52,262 --> 00:01:54,750
anti-sybil checks built in.

23
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,905
Under the hood there are three contracts.

24
00:01:59,905 --> 00:02:06,912
The identity registry stores DID
documents: who controls them, their

25
00:02:06,912 --> 00:02:10,415
metadata, and whether they're active.

26
00:02:10,415 --> 00:02:18,824
The credential manager keeps a list of
trusted issuers, and handles issuing,

27
00:02:18,824 --> 00:02:21,627
revoking and verifying credentials.

28
00:02:21,627 --> 00:02:28,634
And the reputation contract aggregates
scores from registered reporters, and

29
00:02:28,634 --> 00:02:37,042
answers one very useful question: does
this address pass a sybil check?

30
00:02:37,042 --> 00:02:44,750
Each contract does one job, and they can
be used independently.

31
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,115
Most apps won't call the contracts
directly. The TypeScript SDK wraps each

32
00:02:51,115 --> 00:02:53,154
one in a client.

33
00:02:53,154 --> 00:03:00,798
Point it at testnet or mainnet, give it
the three contract IDs, and you can

34
00:03:00,798 --> 00:03:08,952
resolve a DID, verify a credential, or run
a sybil check in a line of code.

35
00:03:08,952 --> 00:03:17,106
There's also a reference React app, the
one you saw at the start, that works with

36
00:03:17,106 --> 00:03:23,731
Freighter and other Stellar wallets, and
an API server for backends that prefer

37
00:03:23,731 --> 00:03:24,750
plain HTTP.

38
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,969
Here's the whole idea in one picture.

39
00:03:29,969 --> 00:03:39,196
An issuer, say a KYC provider, issues a
credential to a subject's address.

40
00:03:39,196 --> 00:03:46,295
The subject shares only the credential ID
with a verifier.

41
00:03:46,295 --> 00:03:54,813
The verifier asks the contract: is this
credential valid, not revoked, not

42
00:03:54,813 --> 00:03:55,522
expired?

43
00:03:55,522 --> 00:04:04,750
The answer is yes or no. The verifier
never needs the passport scan.

44
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:16,837
That pattern covers a lot of ground:
KYC-gated DeFi, reputation for

45
00:04:16,837 --> 00:04:26,522
marketplaces, DAO voting eligibility,
gated communities, and protecting airdrops

46
00:04:26,522 --> 00:04:29,750
from sybil farms.

47
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:39,107
In the next video, the quick start, we'll
deploy all three contracts to testnet and

48
00:04:39,107 --> 00:04:43,964
run the app locally in about ten minutes.

49
00:04:43,964 --> 00:04:51,857
After that we go deep on DIDs, the full
credential lifecycle, and finally

50
00:04:51,857 --> 00:04:56,107
integrating Soroban Identity into your own
app.

51
00:04:56,107 --> 00:04:59,750
See you in the quick start.
